The best approach for your .NET 10 project is to adopt **`System.IO.Abstractions`** — a battle-tested NuGet package that wraps all `File.*`, `Directory.*`, and `Path.*` calls behind an injectable `IFileSystem` interface.

### 1. Install the packages

```bash
dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions
dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers  # in your test project
```

### 2. Inject `IFileSystem` into your class

```csharp
using System.IO.Abstractions;

public class ReportGenerator(IFileSystem fileSystem)
{
    public string GenerateReport(string reportName)
    {
        // ...
        if (!fileSystem.Directory.Exists(outputDir))
            fileSystem.Directory.CreateDirectory(outputDir);

        fileSystem.File.WriteAllText(reportPath, content);
        return reportPath;
    }
}
```

### 3. Register in DI (`Program.cs`)

```csharp
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IFileSystem, FileSystem>();
```

### 4. Test with `MockFileSystem`

```csharp
var mockFs = new MockFileSystem();
mockFs.AddDirectory("/tmp/reports");

var generator = new ReportGenerator(mockFs);
generator.GenerateReport("sales");

Assert.True(mockFs.File.Exists(
    mockFs.AllFiles.Single(f => f.Contains("sales"))));
```

---

> **Bonus:** Your `ReportGenerator` also uses `DateTime.UtcNow`, `Environment.*`, and `Console.*`. For those:
> - **`DateTime.UtcNow`** → inject the built-in `TimeProvider` (register `TimeProvider.System` in DI, use `FakeTimeProvider` in tests)
> - **`Environment.*`** → create a small custom `IEnvironmentProvider` wrapper
> - **`Console.*`** → create a custom `IConsole` wrapper
>
